83 #uuid "f04b535e-e446-5693-b598-2a2ae8017998" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446166 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446156 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446166 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446157 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446166 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446159 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446166 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446160 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446166 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446163 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446166 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446166 1077 "selection" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446166 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446163 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446166 1079 #uuid "45554d58-8407-5972-941e-77d5ad8113b4" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446158 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446161 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446162 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446164 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446165 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446167 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446167 1077 "selection" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446167 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446162 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446167 1079 #uuid "5243b6a3-407c-5377-9662-ad587ee4e1e3" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446167 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446168 1069 "q-178025" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446168 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446168 1072 #uuid "ac6fbe63-89e3-55f0-b383-00653c46559a" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446168 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}+(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446166 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446169 1069 "q-177893" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446169 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446169 1072 #uuid "bffc4e85-37a7-58e6-9707-94d9ac87c744" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446169 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446170 1058 #edb/ref 17592186446171 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446170 1062 #uuid "024575a8-c8f0-5c99-9c1d-bf6941663ece" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446170 1063 #edb/ref 17592186446168 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446171 1062 #uuid "04e86d6f-be2b-5bc9-ac7f-9c117105b2fe" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446171 1063 #edb/ref 17592186284764 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446172 1058 #edb/ref 17592186446173 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446172 1062 #uuid "198647b9-7961-5263-912d-818d3e048db4" 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446172 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198978 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446173 1058 #edb/ref 17592186446174 13194139534971 17592186045417 true] [17592186446173 1062 #uuid "3d1ac785-35a7-523f-9